\section*{Questions in Geometric Group Theory — Q 8.7}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
The linked manuscripts present the following claims by OpenAI: One group of type $F_\infty$ is claimed to contain every finitely presented group, with finitely generated subgroups exactly the recursively presented ones; Such a group is also claimed to embed in a simple group of type $F_\infty$. Together they provide the following support within the scopes described below.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Result 1.} The claimed group \(H\) has type \(F_\infty\), meaning a classifying space with finite skeleton in every dimension, and satisfies
\[
 \forall G\text{ finitely presented},\qquad\exists\iota:G\hookrightarrow H.
\]
The corollary characterizes the finitely generated subgroups embeddable in this same \(H\):
\[
 G\text{ finitely generated}:\qquad G\hookrightarrow H
 \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad G\text{ recursively presented}.
\]
This does not assert embedding of every countable group.

\paragraph{Result 2.} Consider a group \(G\) generated by finitely many elements, with an algorithm deciding its word problem. The assertion supplies a nontrivial simple group \(H\) of type \(F_\infty\) and an embedding
\[
 \iota:G\hookrightarrow H.
\]
Type \(F_\infty\) requires finite skeleta of a classifying space in all dimensions. The decidability condition on \(G\) is part of this theorem.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
A universal finite presentation is inserted into faithful finite diagram envelopes using two systems of idempotents. An ascending-torus construction supplies uniform fillings with finitely many simplex types in each dimension, establishing the claimed higher finiteness of the universal envelope.

Encoded finite diagrams carry faithful actions with controlled stabilizers. A homological device for ascending tori provides fillings in every dimension, and stabilizers of finite type in a highly transitive action assemble the simple overgroup while retaining higher finiteness.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The universal F\_infinity theorem resolves every geometrically F\_n input improvement and in particular FP-group-to-F3 embedding. It does not establish the separate FP2-to-FP3 or TDLC claims, or directly verify the Prüfer embedding clause in the other bundle. The simple F\_infinity overgroup theorem supplies the requested improvement for input groups with decidable word problem. The record includes groups with undecidable word problem and, in the bundles, additional homological/pro-p/TDLC targets. This entry records partial support; complete coverage of the question is not established by this catalogue.

\subsection{Formal verification}
For family 250, the documented entries cover Boone--Higman equivalence, simple $F_\infty$ overgroups for decidable word problems, and one universal $F_\infty$ group. The converse characterization of all its recursively presented subgroups is extra manuscript scope. The listed Comparator files are \texttt{BooneHigman.lean}, \texttt{SimpleOvergroups.lean}, \texttt{UniversalFInfinity.lean}. Named declarations include \texttt{OAI.UniversalFInfinity.universal\_group\_of\_type\_FInfinity}. This site has not executed the Lean code. The scope descriptions do not certify the remaining manuscript arguments.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI attributes these manuscripts to work by an internal model. Its release cautions that arguments without formalization can contain mistakes. This catalogue has not independently checked the proofs; the labels report the mathematical claims attributed to OpenAI.

